Function Get-TrendLine {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
    Calculates a linear trendline for a sequence of numeric data and identifies
    the overall direction as Increasing, Decreasing, or Steady.

    .DESCRIPTION
    Get-TrendLine uses simple linear regression to calculate a best-fit trendline
    for an ordered collection of numeric values.

    The function is intended to be used alongside a graphing or charting function.
    The original data can be plotted as one series and the values returned in the
    Trendline property can be plotted as a second series to show the overall
    direction of the data more clearly.

    One trendline value is generated for every value supplied to the function.
    For example, if the Data parameter contains 30 values, the returned Trendline
    property will also contain 30 values. This allows the original data and the
    calculated trendline to share the same horizontal axis positions.

    The trendline is calculated using the positions of the supplied values as the
    horizontal axis:

        0, 1, 2, 3, and so on.

    This means that the function assumes the observations are supplied in the
    correct chronological or logical order and that they are evenly spaced.

    After calculating the trendline, the function compares its first and last
    values to classify the overall direction:

        Increasing - The final trendline value is more than 105% of the first.
        Decreasing - The final trendline value is less than 95% of the first.
        Steady     - The final trendline value is between 95% and 105% of the first.

    The Steady classification therefore allows for movement of approximately
    five percent in either direction. This prevents small fluctuations from being
    reported as a meaningful upward or downward trend.

    The trend classification is based on the calculated trendline rather than the
    first and last raw data values. This reduces the effect of individual spikes,
    drops, and other short-term variations.

    .PARAMETER Data
    Specifies an ordered collection of numeric values from which the trendline is
    calculated.

    The values should normally be supplied in chronological order, from oldest to
    newest. At least two numeric values are required.

    Null values, text values, and other non-numeric content should be removed or
    converted before calling the function.

    The function is best suited to positive values such as CPU utilisation,
    memory usage, response time, bandwidth consumption, and free disk space.

    .INPUTS
    System.Array

    An array or other collection of numeric values can be supplied to the Data
    parameter.

    .OUTPUTS
    System.Management.Automation.PSCustomObject

    The function returns a custom object containing the following properties:

    Trendline
        An array containing one calculated trendline value for every original data
        value. Each value is rounded to two decimal places.

    Trend
        A string containing one of the following classifications:

            Increasing
            Decreasing
            Steady

    .EXAMPLE
    $data = 20, 22, 21, 25, 27, 29, 30

    $result = Get-TrendLine -Data $data

    $result.Trend
    $result.Trendline

    Calculates a trendline for seven observations. The Trendline property contains
    seven calculated values, and the Trend property describes the overall
    direction.

    .EXAMPLE
    $data = Get-Content '.\cpu-usage.txt' |
        ForEach-Object { [double]$_ }

    $result = Get-TrendLine -Data $data

    [PSCustomObject]@{
        Direction = $result.Trend
        Points    = $result.Trendline.Count
    }

    Reads numeric values from a file, calculates the trendline, and reports the
    direction and number of generated trendline points.

    .EXAMPLE
    $cpuData = 42, 48, 45, 51, 55, 53, 58, 61, 60, 64
    $trendResult = Get-TrendLine -Data $cpuData

    $chartSeries = [PSCustomObject]@{
        ActualData = $cpuData
        Trendline  = $trendResult.Trendline
        Trend      = $trendResult.Trend
    }

    Creates an object containing both the original data and the calculated
    trendline. These two arrays can be passed to a graphing function as separate
    series because they contain the same number of data points.

    .NOTES
    The function performs a least-squares linear regression. It provides a useful
    summary of the overall direction but does not identify seasonal behaviour,
    short-term anomalies, or sudden changes within the data.

    The classification uses a five-percent tolerance:

        Less than 95% of the starting value = Decreasing
        95% through 105%                    = Steady
        More than 105%                      = Increasing

    The function currently rounds trendline values to two decimal places. To
    return whole numbers instead, change:

        [math]::Round($intercept + ($slope * $x), 2)

    to:

        [math]::Round($intercept + ($slope * $x), 0)

    The function requires at least two data values. A single value does not provide
    enough information to calculate a slope.

    The trend calculation divides by the first calculated trendline value.
    Consequently, a first trendline value of zero will cause a division-by-zero
    error. Negative starting values may also produce a trend classification that
    is not meaningful under the percentage-based comparison.

    Large isolated spikes can influence the calculated line. Input data should be
    reviewed for invalid readings or collection errors before the trend is used in
    formal reporting.
    #>
    Param($Data)
    Function Get-Trend {
        param($First, $Last)
        $Percent = (100 / $First) * $Last
        if ($Percent -gt 105) { $Trend = 'Increasing' }
        elseif ($Percent -lt 95) { $Trend = 'Decreasing' }
        else { $Trend = 'Steady' }
        Return $Trend
    }
    # X values are the array positions: 0, 1, 2, 3...
    $xValues = 0..($data.Count - 1)

    # Calculate averages
    $xAverage = ($xValues | Measure-Object -Average).Average
    $yAverage = ($data    | Measure-Object -Average).Average

    # Calculate the slope
    $top = 0
    $bottom = 0

    for ($i = 0; $i -lt $data.Count; $i++) {
        $xDifference = $xValues[$i] - $xAverage
        $yDifference = $data[$i] - $yAverage

        $top += $xDifference * $yDifference
        $bottom += $xDifference * $xDifference
    }

    $slope = $top / $bottom

    # Calculate where the trendline begins
    $intercept = $yAverage - ($slope * $xAverage)

    # Generate one trendline value for each original data value
    $trendline = foreach ($x in $xValues) {
        [math]::Round($intercept + ($slope * $x), 2)
    }
    $Trend = Get-Trend -First ($trendline | select-object -First 1) -Last  ($trendline | select-object -Last 1)
    Return [PSCustomObject]@{
        Trendline = $trendline 
        Trend     = $Trend
    } 
}
